Summary Classification of Revision 1.1 Findings & Resolutions
Confirmed Defects Resolved in Design
Premature Semaphore Permit Release (Revision 1.0 Defect): Releasing concurrency permits inside request.raw.once('aborted') or reply.raw.once('close') breaks the concurrency cap whenever Argon2 hashing, PDF rendering, PostgreSQL queries, or Daraja STK Push calls continue executing after client socket disconnect. Permits are now bound exclusively to try { await fn(signal) } finally { permit.release() }; client disconnect/timeout aborts AbortController only and never directly releases an active permit (§4).
Conflicting CSRF Configuration Switches (Revision 1.0 Defect): CSRF_REQUIRE_TOKEN, CSRF_STRICT_MODE, and CSRF_ALLOW_HEADER_ONLY_FALLBACK created contradictory states and test-mode weakening risk. All three boolean switches are removed; strict conjunctive Origin + X-CSRF-Token enforcement is always active in development, test, and production (§3).
PostgreSQL JSONB::text Scientific-Notation & Invalid-Unicode Gaps (Revision 1.0 Defect): JavaScript JSON.stringify(5e-324) is 6 bytes ("5e-324"), whereas PostgreSQL octet_length(('5e-324'::jsonb)::text) expands via numeric_out to 326 bytes ("0.000...005"), able to blow past a 296-byte safety buffer and trigger chk_audit_logs_context_bytes (23514). In addition, PostgreSQL jsonb rejects \u0000 and unpaired UTF-16 surrogates with SQLSTATE 22P05. Both are resolved via input normalization (toWellFormed() + NUL stripping) and a closed-form numeric_out upper-bound byte estimator proven >= octet_length(col::text) across all normalized inputs (§7).
Retention Cursor Type Mismatch for system.rate_limit_buckets: RetentionService.runWorkerLoop (src/services/retention.service.ts:499-531) selects column id and writes last_cursor_id into governance.data_retention_actions.last_cursor_id, which is typed UUID NULL (drizzle/0017_audit_and_retention.sql:89). Migration 0024 therefore defines "id" UUID PRIMARY KEY DEFAULT gen_random_uuid() alongside UNIQUE ("bucket_key"), preserving ON CONFLICT ("bucket_key") while maintaining 100% UUID cursor compatibility with RetentionService (§5.4).
search.ts Ad-Hoc Auth Cookie & Error Codes (src/routes/api/v1/search.ts:106-130, 306, 318): Confirmed live defect using elimu_session / Authorization: Bearer, 401 UNAUTHORIZED (instead of UNAUTHENTICATED), and verbose permission-string leakage in 403 messages.
calendar.ts Cookie Clear Attribute Drift (src/routes/api/v1/calendar.ts:277): reply.clearCookie('calendar_session', { path: '/' }) omits httpOnly, sameSite, and secure flags set during issuance (lines 193-199).
Security Design Gaps Closed
Pre-Auth CSRF Bootstrap Without Client Cookie Inspection: Browser JavaScript never reads HttpOnly cookies. GET /api/v1/auth/csrf binds stateless HMAC-SHA256 tokens server-side to the SHA-256 digest of the active HttpOnly credential/session cookie (session_token, calendar_session, rf_sid, elimu_anon_sid) or issues an opaque HttpOnly; SameSite=Lax pre-auth context cookie (csrf_ctx) prior to /auth/login and /auth/register, rotating X-CSRF-Token automatically whenever session cookies rotate (§3.2).
Token-Bucket Early Quota Reset Prevention & Multi-Dimensional Atomicity: Enforced expires_at >= updated_at + full_bucket_refill_duration + 60s in both TypeScript and PostgreSQL, and replaced sequential multi-bucket checks with a single-roundtrip deadlock-free PostgreSQL function system.consume_rate_limit_buckets(...) that locks sorted bucket hashes via pg_advisory_xact_lock, uses a single clock_timestamp(), prevents BIGINT overflow/rounding drift, and performs zero writes when any dimension (IP or email) rejects (§5).
Database Pool Protection (DATABASE_POOL_MAX = 10): Public read endpoints, search queries, and high-volume analytics ingestion use Tier 1 in-memory bounded-LRU rate limiting only (zero per-request PostgreSQL writes); Tier 2 PostgreSQL rate limiting is scoped strictly to authentication, registration, commerce/payment, downloads/exports, and state-changing user/admin mutations with a 250ms pool-saturation circuit guard (§6).
CORS Preflight Cache Safety & HSTS Rollout Safety: OPTIONS preflights emit Vary: Origin, Access-Control-Request-Method, Access-Control-Request-Headers with Cache-Control: private, max-age=600 on allowed preflights (no-store on denied preflights), while HSTS removes includeSubDomains by default, requires verified HTTPS + production mode (RFC 6797 §7.2), and defaults HSTS_MAX_AGE_SECONDS to 300 (5 minutes) for safe staged rollout (§8, §9).
Existing Controls Verified & Preserved
Wave 1 resolveEffectiveTrustProxy (src/app.ts:81-128), request-ID regex (src/app.ts:74, 203-209), M-Pesa MpesaWebhookRateLimiter + 32 KiB limit + raw decimal lexeme parser (src/routes/middleware/mpesa-webhook-guard.ts), AuditService.logEvent(input, tx) (src/services/audit.service.ts:127, 135), Migration 0017 append-only triggers & 4,096/2,048-byte CHECK constraints, and Wave 2 visibility/RBAC SQL predicates remain intact.
