3.2 Token Lifecycle Specification (Zero Client Reading of HttpOnly Cookies)
Classification: Security design gap closed.
All existing session cookies in the repository are HttpOnly:
session_token (env.SESSION_COOKIE_NAME, src/routes/api/v1/auth.ts:154-160, 219-225, 344-350)
calendar_session (src/routes/api/v1/calendar.ts:193-199)
rf_sid (env.FEEDBACK_SESSION_COOKIE_NAME, src/routes/api/v1/feedback.ts:183-189)
elimu_anon_sid (src/routes/api/v1/recommendations.ts:266)
Browser JavaScript cannot and never reads any cookie to construct or verify CSRF tokens. Instead, the server binds CSRF tokens to the SHA-256 digest of the raw HttpOnly cookie received in the Cookie request header—or, when no session cookie exists yet, to a dedicated opaque HttpOnly pre-authentication context cookie (csrf_ctx).
1. Principal Subject Binding Hierarchy (resolveCsrfSubject)
Given request.cookies, the server computes subjectBinding with zero database queries:
If cookies[env.SESSION_COOKIE_NAME] (session_token) is non-empty:
subjectBinding = "auth:" + sha256Hex(rawSessionToken)
Else if cookies.calendar_session is non-empty:
subjectBinding = "cal:" + sha256Hex(rawCalendarSession)
Else if cookies[env.FEEDBACK_SESSION_COOKIE_NAME] (rf_sid) is non-empty:
subjectBinding = "fb:" + sha256Hex(rawRfSid)
Else if cookies.elimu_anon_sid is non-empty:
subjectBinding = "anon:" + sha256Hex(rawAnonSid)
Else if cookies.csrf_ctx is non-empty:
subjectBinding = "pre:" + sha256Hex(rawCsrfCtx)
Else (unauthenticated visitor with zero cookies):
During GET /api/v1/auth/csrf bootstrap: server generates rawCsrfCtx = crypto.randomBytes(16).toString('hex') (128-bit CSPRNG), sets cookie csrf_ctx (HttpOnly: true, SameSite: 'lax', Secure: effectiveNodeEnv === 'production', Path: '/', Max-Age: env.CSRF_TOKEN_TTL_SECONDS), and binds to "pre:" + sha256Hex(rawCsrfCtx).
During an unsafe mutation (POST/PUT/PATCH/DELETE) arriving with no csrf_ctx and no session cookie: subjectBinding = null → rejected with 403 CSRF_TOKEN_INVALID.
2. Token Wire Format & Cryptographic Construction
code
Text
v1.<issuedAtUnixSec>.<nonceHex32>.<hmacHex64>
issuedAtUnixSec: 10-digit integer Unix epoch seconds (Math.floor(Date.now() / 1000)).
nonceHex32: crypto.randomBytes(16).toString('hex') (128 bits of CSPRNG per-token randomness).
hmacHex64: lowercase hex of HMAC-SHA256(CSRF_SECRET, "v1|" + subjectBinding + "|" + issuedAtUnixSec + "|" + nonceHex32).
3. Bootstrap Endpoint (GET /api/v1/auth/csrf)
Route: GET /api/v1/auth/csrf in src/routes/api/v1/auth.ts.
Headers: Cache-Control: private, no-store, max-age=0, Pragma: no-cache, X-CSRF-Token: <token>.
Body: { data: { csrfToken: "<token>", expiresInSeconds: env.CSRF_TOKEN_TTL_SECONDS } }.
Pre-Auth Bootstrap Flow (/auth/login & /auth/register):
Unauthenticated browser calls GET /api/v1/auth/csrf.
Server sets Set-Cookie: csrf_ctx=<128-bit-hex>; Path=/; HttpOnly; SameSite=Lax[; Secure] and returns { data: { csrfToken } } bound to pre:<sha256(csrf_ctx)>.
Browser submits POST /api/v1/auth/login or POST /api/v1/auth/register with Origin: <allowed_origin>, automatic browser Cookie: csrf_ctx=..., and header X-CSRF-Token: <csrfToken>.
Server verifies Origin allowlist and verifies X-CSRF-Token against pre:<sha256(csrf_ctx)> (stopping login-CSRF / account-fixation attacks).
4. Rotation Across Session Transitions
An onSend hook in src/routes/middleware/csrf-guard.ts inspects outgoing Set-Cookie mutations whenever a route sets, rotates, or clears session_token, calendar_session, or rf_sid:
On POST /api/v1/auth/register, POST /api/v1/auth/login, and POST /api/v1/auth/change-password: auth.ts sets a newly generated session_token; csrf-guard clears csrf_ctx (with matching HttpOnly, SameSite=Lax, Secure, Path=/ attributes) and emits a fresh X-CSRF-Token response header bound to auth:<sha256(newSessionToken)>. Any pre-login pre: token or prior-session auth: token is immediately invalid on the next request because resolveCsrfSubject now resolves to the new session_token.
On POST /api/v1/auth/logout: auth.ts clears session_token; csrf-guard mints a fresh csrf_ctx cookie and emits a fresh X-CSRF-Token response header bound to pre:<sha256(newCsrfCtx)>. Notably, if a user clicks logout after their session_token has already expired in PostgreSQL, POST /api/v1/auth/logout still succeeds cleanly because resolveCsrfSubject hashes the raw cookie value without requiring an active DB session row.
On POST /api/v1/calendar/session, DELETE /api/v1/calendar/session, and POST /api/v1/resources/:id/feedback: When an unauthenticated visitor transitions from csrf_ctx to calendar_session or rf_sid (or clears calendar_session), csrf-guard automatically emits the newly bound X-CSRF-Token in the response header so subsequent mutations require zero extra round-trips (clients may also refresh at any time via GET /api/v1/auth/csrf).
5. Constant-Time Validation Rules
Read single x-csrf-token header (typeof header === 'string', length <= 128; arrays or missing header fail immediately with 403 CSRF_TOKEN_MISSING).
Match strict format /^v1\.(\d{10,11})\.([0-9a-f]{32})\.([0-9a-f]{64})$/ (mismatch → 403 CSRF_TOKEN_INVALID).
Verify integer timestamp: issuedAt <= nowSec + 60 (60s clock-skew allowance) and nowSec - issuedAt <= env.CSRF_TOKEN_TTL_SECONDS (expired → 403 CSRF_TOKEN_EXPIRED).
Decode hmacHex64 into a 32-byte Buffer. Compute expected 32-byte HMAC digest using CSRF_SECRET (and, if that fails and CSRF_SECRET_PREVIOUS is configured, using CSRF_SECRET_PREVIOUS), comparing fixed 32-byte buffers strictly via crypto.timingSafeEqual(expectedBuf, candidateBuf). Mismatch → 403 CSRF_TOKEN_INVALID.
