4. WP4 — Semaphore Lifecycle & Premature-Release Fix
4.1 Why Premature Release Breaks Concurrency Control & Corrected Lifecycle
Classification: Confirmed defect in Revision 1.0 design resolved.
Defect in Revision 1.0: Attaching permit.release() to request.raw.once('aborted') or reply.raw.once('close') releases the semaphore slot the instant a client closes its TCP socket, even when the server's in-flight argon2.hash(), pdf-lib export, PostgreSQL query, or Daraja STK Push continues executing on the Node.js event loop, libuv threadpool, or database connection pool. An attacker opening N connections, triggering heavy work, and immediately resetting TCP sockets (RST) could spawn unbounded concurrent Argon2/PDF/DB operations while the semaphore counter reads 0 active permits.
Corrected Invariant (BoundedConcurrencyRegistry.runWithPermit):
Queued Waiters (Before Permit Acquisition): While waiting in the bounded FIFO queue (activePermits === maxConcurrent), the waiter has not started work and holds no permit. If request disconnects (request.raw close before reply.sent) or queueWaitTimeoutMs expires while still queued, the waiter is spliced from the queue immediately, its timer/listener is cleaned up, and runWithPermit rejects (499/503 SERVER_BUSY) with zero permit counter change.
Active Holders (After Permit Acquisition): Once a permit (and optional per-actor slot) is granted:
code
Ts
const abortController = new AbortController();
const onDisconnect = () => {
  if (!reply.sent && policy.abortOnClientDisconnect) {
    abortController.abort(new Error('CLIENT_DISCONNECTED'));
