7. WP7 — PostgreSQL JSONB::text Byte-Measurement Validation & Conservative Guarantee
7.1 Exact Divergences Between JS Serialization and PostgreSQL octet_length(col::text)
Classification: Confirmed defect in naive JS byte heuristics identified and resolved.
Migration 0017 (drizzle/0017_audit_and_retention.sql:33-34) enforces hard PostgreSQL CHECK constraints:
CONSTRAINT chk_audit_logs_actor_snapshot_bytes CHECK (octet_length(actor_snapshot::text) <= 2048)
CONSTRAINT chk_audit_logs_context_bytes CHECK (octet_length(context::text) <= 4096)
PostgreSQL's jsonb_out (col::text) differs from JavaScript Buffer.byteLength(JSON.stringify(x), 'utf8') in four concrete ways:
Whitespace After Punctuation (": " and ", "): Non-empty objects add +1 byte after every : and +1 byte after every ,; non-empty arrays add +1 byte after every ,. Empty {} and [] remain 2 bytes. For an object with 80 short keys ({"a":1,"b":2,...}), JSON.stringify undercounts by 159 bytes.
Scientific-Notation IEEE-754 Numbers (numeric_out Fixed-Point Expansion — CRITICAL): PostgreSQL jsonb stores numbers as numeric and prints them in fixed-point decimal without scientific notation.
JSON.stringify(1e-7) is "1e-7" (4 bytes), whereas SELECT octet_length(('1e-7'::jsonb)::text) is octet_length('0.0000001') = 9 bytes (+5 bytes).
JSON.stringify(5e-324) (Number.MIN_VALUE) is "5e-324" (6 bytes), whereas SELECT octet_length(('5e-324'::jsonb)::text) is 326 bytes (+320 bytes per number!). Just two small scientific floats in context would defeat a naive 296-byte buffer (3,800 vs 4,096) and crash chk_audit_logs_context_bytes!
JSON.stringify(1e+25) is "1e+25" (5 bytes), whereas PG jsonb::text expands to "10000000000000000000000000" (26 bytes).
PostgreSQL jsonb 22P05 Rejections on \u0000 and Lone UTF-16 Surrogates: Even though JSON.stringify("\u0000") ("\\u0000") and JSON.stringify("\uD800") ("\\ud800") are valid strings in V8, PostgreSQL jsonb throws SQLSTATE 22P05 on NUL code points (\u0000 cannot be converted to text) and unpaired UTF-16 surrogates!
Non-JSON Runtime Types (BigInt, Date, -0, NaN/Infinity, Buffer, Circular Graphs): JSON.stringify(1n) throws TypeError; Buffer.from(...) serializes to {"type":"Buffer","data":[...]} (~4x byte blow-up); -0 becomes 0 ("0" in both JS and PG). Note that PG jsonb key sorting by (key_byte_len, key_bytes) is a pure permutation of unique object entries and changes octet_length by 0 bytes.
